<h3>Two kinds of knowledge, and nothing else</h3>
<p>Section 4 opens with one of the most powerful moves in the history of philosophy. Hume divides all objects of human reason — everything we can possibly think or know — into exactly two classes. There is no third option.</p>
<p><strong>Relations of ideas</strong> are propositions that are necessarily true or false in virtue of the meanings of the terms involved. Mathematics is the clearest example: “the interior angles of a triangle sum to 180 degrees” is true, and its truth can be established by pure reasoning, without going out and measuring any actual triangles. The contrary — that they sum to 200 degrees — is not just false but <em>inconceivable</em>. Logic works the same way: if all humans are mortal and Socrates is human, then Socrates is mortal is necessarily true. You cannot coherently deny it.</p>
<p><strong>Matters of fact</strong>, by contrast, are propositions about how the world actually is. “The sun rises in the east” is a matter of fact. Its contrary — that it rises in the west — is not a logical contradiction. It is conceivable. It just happens to be false. All factual claims about the world, from the most mundane to the most scientific, fall into this category. And crucially: their truth cannot be established by reasoning alone. We have to go look.</p>
